28 | 8-12 GB | **16 GB** | 24 GB | 32-256 GB |\n| 2030 | 12 GB | **16-24 GB** | 24-32 GB | 64-512 GB |\n\n**Source:** Analyst forecasts from multiple outlets. **Tier: C** (projections, not confirmed)\n\n---\n\n### Evidence Table\n\n| Source | Key Quote/Claim | Tier |\n|--------|----------------|------|\n| nvidia.com (RTX 50 specs) | RTX 5090=24GB, 5080=16GB, 5070/Ti=12GB, 5060/5050=8GB GDDR7 | A |\n| Tom's Hardware / TechRadar | RTX 5060 8GB \"considered entry-level for local LLM inference\" and \"severely limits\" AI model capabilities | B |\n| AMD Strix Halo specs | Up to 128GB LPDDR5X unified, 256 GB/s, RDNA 3.5, ~96GB allocatable as GPU memory | A |\n| Apple.com (M5 family) | M5 Max up to 128GB, M5 Ultra up to 512GB unified; M5 Ultra 1.2 TB/s bandwidth | A |\n| Windows Central / TechRadar | RTX Spark N1X: ARM SoC, Blackwell GPU, up to 128GB unified, Oct 2026 launch | B |\n| Multiple NPU analyses | \"NPU does not inherently reduce the amount of memory required to run an AI model\" | A/B |\n| VRLATech / TweakTown roadmap | Rubin→Feynman→Rosa Feynman (2027-2029); 16GB becoming mid-range standard | B |\n| PCGamesN / Igor's Lab | GDDR7 costs rising due to HBM allocation; \"no meaningful relief until 2028\" | B |\n| Hardware review consensus | Models exceeding VRAM \"spill\" causes \"dramatic performance cliff\" — hundreds of tok/s to single digits | A/B |\n| Apple/NVIDIA benchmark comparisons | RTX 5090 delivers 2-3× tokens/sec vs M4 Max for models fitting in 24GB VRAM; M4 Max wins for 70B+ models | B |\n| Analyst forecasts (multiple) | Mid-range laptops expected to standardize at 16GB VRAM by 2028; 24GB for high-end | C |\n\n---\n\n### Counter-Evidence\n\n1. **\"8GB is fine\" argument:** Supporters argue that at 1080p gaming and with quantized 7B-9B parameter models, 8GB VRAM is adequate. For casual AI experimentation and simple chat tasks, 8GB works. However, this is increasingly seen as a minority position even among budget buyers, and it fails for complex reasoning, agents, or long-context workloads.\n\n2. **Unified memory bandwidth is lower:** While Apple/AMD Strix Halo/NVIDIA Spark offer huge memory pools, their bandwidth (120-546 GB/s) is lower than dedicated GDDR7 VRAM (~896 GB/s on RTX 5090). For models that fit in 24GB, dedicated VRAM is 2-3× faster in tokens/sec. The unified memory advantage is capacity, not speed.\n\n3. **NPU hype is overstated:** Despite marketing around 50-85 TOPS NPUs, the software ecosystem for NPU-based LLM inference is immature. Most local AI software is optimized for GPUs (CUDA) or Apple Metal/MLX. NPUs handle background tasks but cannot run full LLMs.\n\n4. **Memory pricing may delay VRAM increases:** GDDR7 supply constraints and rising costs mean that the hoped-for VRAM increases in mid-range laptops may be delayed. 16GB in mid-range may not arrive until 2028 rather than 2027.\n\n---\n\n### Gaps\n\n1. **Exact RTX 60-series VRAM specs:** Completely unconfirmed — all speculation. NVIDIA has not announced consumer Rubin GPUs.\n2. **AMD RDNA 4 mobile discrete GPUs:** No confirmed discrete mobile RDNA 4 products; AMD's mobile strategy pivoted to APUs (Strix Halo).\n3. **RTX Spark real-world AI benchmarks:** Product announced but not shipping yet (Oct 2026) — no independent reviews.\n4. **GDDR7 pricing forecasts beyond 2027:** Highly uncertain; depends on AI datacenter demand vs consumer GPU allocation.\n5. **Feynman/Rosa architecture consumer details:** Pure roadmap; no specs available for 2028-2030 consumer products.\n6. **M6/M7 Apple Silicon details:** M6 just starting to appear (Mac mini, Aug 2026); M6 Pro/Max may be skipped in favor of M7 (2027). No confirmed specs.\n\n---\n\n### Summary for the Parent Research\n\n**For someone buying a laptop in September 2026 for a 5-year useful life (to ~2031):**\n\n- **Minimum viable for AI:** 12GB dedicated VRAM (RTX 5070-class) or 24GB+ unified memory (Apple M5 Pro or AMD Strix Halo)\n- **Recommended sweet spot:** 16GB dedicated VRAM (RTX 5080-class, ~$2,000+) or 48-64GB unified memory (M5 Pro/Max)\n- **Future-proof for AI:** 24GB dedicated VRAM (RTX 5090, ~$2,500+) or 128GB unified memory (M5 Max)\n- **The paradigm shift:** Unified memory platforms (Apple, AMD Strix Halo, NVIDIA RTX Spark) are becoming the preferred architecture for local AI because they eliminate the VRAM wall entirely, trading raw speed for capacity\n- **NPUs are irrelevant** to VRAM needs — they don't help run LLMs\n- **16GB VRAM is expected to become the mid-range standard by 2028**, but memory supply constraints may delay this\n- **8GB VRAM is already insufficient** for meaningful local AI work in 2026 and will be obsolete for AI by 2028","Recipient":"c9e10e04-5086-4628-b678-26c6a5b93a9a","toolAction":"Sending research findings","toolSummary":"Complete VRAM research report"}}]}
